Rate limits should be tied to the threat and resource: login attempts need protection against credential stuffing, while expensive API operations may need per-user, per-tenant, IP, or global limits. Enforce them at a trusted edge or shared service so multiple application instances see consistent state, and use a monotonic window or token-bucket design appropriate to the traffic. Return HTTP 429 when the client is being limited, avoid revealing whether an account exists, and include a safe retry hint when useful. Combine throttling with progressive delay, monitoring, alerting, and account-risk controls; a single IP-only limit is easy to evade and can punish shared networks.

```ts caption="Application-level response after a shared limiter denies a request."
if (!limiter.allow(`${tenantId}:${userId}`)) {
  res.status(429).setHeader("Retry-After", "10").end();
  return;
}
```
